5	   "where": "BDY-31 (line 205: \"`post_restore` sets `pos_prev := pos_curr` (Pipeline C5, SL-15 item 8)\") vs Cross-References 8.3 Pipeline row (line 328: \"Body relies on the kernel's `snap_baseline` ... no `post_restore` `previous := current` (C5)\"); also BDY-6 (line 137), BDY-14 (line 165), BDY-26 (lines 193 and 490), and AC-BDY-87 (line 1236: \"`pos_prev == pos_curr` for every actor\")",
6	   "issue": "The ADR-0008 sync made the Pipeline row say Body no longer repeats `previous := current` in `post_restore`, and the row-964 table says the camera trigger is \"no longer `post_restore`\". BDY-31 still has `post_restore` set `pos_prev := pos_curr` and cites Pipeline C5 as the reason, and BDY-6, BDY-14, BDY-26 and AC-BDY-87 still depend on that write. An implementer cannot tell whether Body's `post_restore` writes `pos_prev` and `world_prev` or leaves it to the kernel `snap_baseline`. The same ambiguity covers whether the PC-18 test 10 fixture and AC-BDY-87 assert a Body-side write or a kernel-side one.",
7	   "fix": "State one rule and use it in all places. Suggested wording: Body's own `post_restore` still derives `pos_prev := pos_curr` (and resets `world_prev`), because `pos_prev` is a non-persisted derived field and the restore ACs assert it. That write is Body-internal derivation and is NOT the camera-continuity mechanism. Camera and presentation restore continuity is solely the kernel `snap_baseline` plus the camera CUT on `load_seq` change (ADR-0008 section 6.1). Then (a) in BDY-31, drop \"(Pipeline C5, SL-15 item 8)\" as the justification and cite BDY-6 instead; (b) reword line 328 to \"no Pipeline-driven or camera-driven `post_restore` `previous := current`; Body's derived `pos_prev` reset is block-internal, BDY-6\"; (c) if you instead intend the kernel to own the `pos_prev` reset for interpolated blocks, remove the `post_restore` writes and the `pos_prev == pos_curr` post-restore assertions in BDY-6, BDY-14, BDY-26, BDY-31 and AC-BDY-87, and say which kernel hook does the reset."
8	  }
9	 ]
